#' Leadership in directional correlation delay
#'
#' `leader_direction_delay` calculates the directional correlation delay within
#' dyads to identify leadership patterns. The function expects a distance based
#' edge-list generated by `edge_dist`, a `data.table` with relocation data,
#' individual identifiers and a window argument. The window argument is used to
#' specify the temporal window within which to measure the directional
#' correlation delay. Relocation data should be in two columns representing the
#' X and Y coordinates.
#'
#' The function expects a distance based edge-list from `edge_dist` with columns
#' 'ID1' and 'ID2' indicating individuals and column 'dyadID' indicating the
#' dyad ID from `dyad_id`.
#'
#' The `edge` must be a `data.table`. If your data is a `data.frame`, you can
#' convert it by reference using [data.table::setDT()] or by reassigning using
#' [data.table::data.table()].
#'
#' `leader_direction_delay` respects the equalities described in Nagy et al.
#' 2010 (\doi{doi:10.1038/nature08891}) where
#'
#' \deqn{C_{ij}(\tau) = C_{ji}(-\tau)}
#'
#' and
#'
#' \deqn{\tau^{*}_{ij} = -\tau^{*}_{ji}}
#'
#' See internal tests that confirm these equalities are respected.
#'
#' @return `leader_direction_delay` returns columns 'ID1', 'ID2' and 'dyadID'
#'   from edges, the column 'direction_delay' indicating the directional delay
#'   time (a sequence from -window to window e.g. for `window = 2` the
#'   'direction_delay' column will include -2, -1, 0, 1, 2 for each dyad), the
#'   column 'direction_cor' incidating the mean correlation between directions
#'   for ID1 at the focal timegroup and ID2 at the directional delay time in
#'   'direction_delay'. The column 'max_direction_cor' is also returned to
#'   indicate the maximum direction correlation for each dyad. The columns
#'   optionally provided in `splitBy` are used to restrict the calculations
#'   within subsets of the input edges. For example, if a column named 'month'
#'   is passed to `splitBy` (`splitBy = 'month'`), the directional correlation
#'   delay and directional delay time will be calculated for each dyad within
#'   months.
#'
#'  Note: since `leader_direction_delay` returns an aggregation of the input
#'  `edges`, the output needs to be reassigned unlike some other
#'  `spatsoc` functions like `dyad_id`. See details in
#'  [FAQ](https://docs.ropensci.org/spatsoc/articles/faq.html).
#'
#' @inheritParams edge_delay
#' @param splitBy (optional) character string or vector of grouping column
#'   name(s) upon which the directional correlation delay will be calculated.
#'   For example, if a column named 'month' is passed to splitBy
#'   (`splitBy = c('month')`), the output directional delay will be
#'   calculated within each month.
#'
#' @export
#' @seealso `edge_delay`
#' @family Leadership functions
#' @family Direction functions
#'
#' @references
#'
#' The directional correlation delay is defined in Nagy et al. 2010
#' (\doi{doi:10.1038/nature08891}). Nagy's version measures the global
#' directional correlation delay within dyads across sampling periods and is
#' provided by the function [leader_direction_delay()]. Quera et al. 2023
#' (\doi{doi:10.1111/jfb.15315}) adapted the directional correlation delay from
#' the global measure described in Nagy et al. 2010 to a momentary measure of
#' directional correlation delay that determines the temporal delay that
#' maximizes the direction correlation within dyads at each time step. See
#' [edge_delay()] for the momentary measure defined in Quera et al. 2023.
#'
#' See examples of measuring leadership using the directional correlation
#' delay:
#'  * \doi{doi:10.1016/j.anbehav.2013.07.005}
#'  * \doi{doi:10.1073/pnas.1305552110}
#'  * \doi{doi:10.1126/science.aap7781}
#'  * \doi{doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1003446}
#'
#' @examples
#' # Load data.table
#' library(data.table)
#' \dontshow{data.table::setDTthreads(1)}
#'
#' # Read example data
#' DT <- fread(system.file("extdata", "DT.csv", package = "spatsoc"))
#'
#' # Select only individuals A, B, C for this example
#' DT <- DT[ID %in% c('A', 'B', 'C')]
#'
#' # Cast the character column to POSIXct
#' DT[, datetime := as.POSIXct(datetime, tz = 'UTC')]
#'
#' # Temporal grouping
#' group_times(DT, datetime = 'datetime', threshold = '20 minutes')
#'
#' # Calculate direction
#' direction_step(
#'   DT = DT,
#'   id = 'ID',
#'   coords = c('X', 'Y'),
#'   crs = 32736
#' )
#'
#' # Distance based edge-list generation
#' edges <- edge_dist(
#'   DT,
#'   threshold = 100,
#'   id = 'ID',
#'   coords = c('X', 'Y'),
#'   timegroup = 'timegroup',
#'   returnDist = TRUE,
#'   fillNA = FALSE
#' )
#'
#' # Generate dyad id
#' dyad_id(edges, id1 = 'ID1', id2 = 'ID2')
#'
#' # Generate fusion id
#' fusion_id(edges, threshold = 100)
#'
#' # Leadership from directional correlation delay
#' lead_delay <- leader_direction_delay(
#'   edges = edges,
#'   DT = DT,
#'   window = 3,
#'   id = 'ID'
#' )
#' print(lead_delay)
#'
#' # Subset to maximum values of the directional correlation delay for each dyad
#' max_lead_delay <- lead_delay[(max_direction_cor)]
#' print(max_lead_delay)
#'
#' # Use a correlation threshold to subset directional delay values
#' cor_threshold <- 0.7
#' max_lead_delay[direction_cor > cor_threshold]
#'
#' # Or, using the new geometry interface
#' get_geometry(DT, coords = c('X', 'Y'), crs = 32736)
#' direction_step(DT, id = 'ID')
#' edges <- edge_dist(DT, threshold = 100, id = 'ID', timegroup = 'timegroup', returnDist = TRUE)
#' dyad_id(edges, id = 'ID1', id2 = 'ID2')
#' fusion_id(edges, threshold = 100)
#' lead_delay <- leader_direction_delay(
#'   edges = edges,
#'   DT = DT,
#'   window = 3,
#'   id = 'ID'
#' )
#' print(lead_delay)
#'
#' # Subset to maximum values of the directional correlation delay for each dyad
#' max_lead_delay <- lead_delay[(max_direction_cor)]
#' print(max_lead_delay)
#'
#' # Use a correlation threshold to subset directional delay values
#' cor_threshold <- 0.7
#' max_lead_delay[direction_cor > cor_threshold]
leader_direction_delay <- function(
  edges,
  DT,
  window = NULL,
  id = NULL,
  direction = 'direction',
  min_nobs = 0,
  splitBy = NULL
) {
  # due to NSE notes in R CMD check
  . <- fusionID <- ID1 <- ID2 <- dyadID <- timegroup <- direction_ID1 <-
    direction_ID2 <- sufficient_nobs_ID1 <- sufficient_nobs_ID2 <-
      N_by_fusion <- DT_direction_column <- max_direction_cor <- wt_mn <- NULL

  assert_not_null(DT)
  assert_is_data_table(DT)
  assert_not_null(edges)
  assert_is_data_table(edges)
  assert_not_null(id)

  check_cols_edges <- c('ID1', 'ID2', 'timegroup', splitBy)
  assert_are_colnames(edges, check_cols_edges)

  check_cols_DT <- c(id, 'timegroup', direction)
  assert_are_colnames(DT, check_cols_DT)

  assert_not_null(window)
  assert_inherits(window, 'numeric')
  assert_relation(window, `>`, 0)

  assert_col_inherits(DT, 'timegroup', 'integer')
  assert_col_inherits(edges, 'timegroup', 'integer')

  assert_not_null(min_nobs)
  assert_inherits(min_nobs, 'numeric')
  assert_relation(min_nobs, `>=`, 0)

  assert_are_colnames(edges, 'dyadID', ', did you run dyad_id?')
  assert_are_colnames(edges, 'fusionID', ', did you run fusion_id?')

  assert_col_radians(DT, direction, ', did you use direction_step?')

  # "Forward": all edges ID1 -> ID2
  drop_nas <- edges[
    !(is.na(fusionID) | is.na(ID1) | is.na(ID2) | is.na(dyadID))
  ]

  forward <- drop_nas[ID1 == tstrsplit(dyadID, '-')[[1L]]]

  tg_matching <- forward[,
    {
      tg <- seq(min(timegroup), max(timegroup))
      list(
        timegroup = tg,
        ID1 = rep(ID1, length.out = length(tg)),
        ID2 = rep(ID2, length.out = length(tg))
      )
    },
    by = c('dyadID', 'fusionID', splitBy)
  ]

  tg_matching[
    DT,
    direction_ID1 := units::drop_units(DT_direction_column),
    on = c(paste0('ID1 == ', id), 'timegroup == timegroup'),
    env = list(DT_direction_column = paste0('i.', direction))
  ]
  tg_matching[
    DT,
    direction_ID2 := units::drop_units(DT_direction_column),
    on = c(paste0('ID2 == ', id), 'timegroup == timegroup'),
    env = list(DT_direction_column = paste0('i.', direction))
  ]

  seq_tau <- -window:window

  if (min_nobs > 0) {
    tg_matching[,
      c('sufficient_nobs_ID1', 'sufficient_nobs_ID2') := {
        window_dir1 <- do.call(
          cbind,
          shift(direction_ID1, seq_tau, type = 'lead')
        )
        window_dir2 <- do.call(
          cbind,
          shift(direction_ID2, seq_tau, type = 'lead')
        )

        n_obs_ID1 <- apply(window_dir1, MARGIN = 1, FUN = function(x) {
          sum(!is.na(x))
        })
        n_obs_ID2 <- apply(window_dir2, MARGIN = 1, FUN = function(x) {
          sum(!is.na(x))
        })
        list(n_obs_ID1 >= min_nobs, n_obs_ID2 >= min_nobs)
      },
      by = c('fusionID', splitBy)
    ]
    tg_matching[!(sufficient_nobs_ID1), direction_ID1 := NA]
    tg_matching[!(sufficient_nobs_ID2), direction_ID2 := NA]
  }

  tg_matching[, N_by_fusion := .N, by = fusionID]

  means <- tg_matching[
    N_by_fusion > 1,
    {
      shift_tg <- do.call(cbind, shift(timegroup, seq_tau, type = 'lead'))

      shift_dir_ID2 <- do.call(
        cbind,
        shift(direction_ID2, seq_tau, type = 'lead')
      )
      dif_dir_ID1 <- cos(direction_ID1 - shift_dir_ID2)

      nobs_ID1 <- apply(dif_dir_ID1, 2, function(x) sum(!is.na(x)))

      mean_ID1 <- apply(dif_dir_ID1, 2, mean, na.rm = TRUE)

      list(
        mean_ID1,
        nobs_ID1,
        del = seq_tau
      )
    },
    by = c('fusionID', splitBy)
  ]

  means[
    forward,
    c('ID1', 'ID2', 'dyadID') := .(ID1, ID2, dyadID),
    on = 'fusionID'
  ]

  out <- means[,
    .(wt_mn = stats::weighted.mean(mean_ID1, nobs_ID1, na.rm = TRUE)),
    by = c('ID1', 'ID2', 'dyadID', 'del', splitBy)
  ]

  out[,
    max_direction_cor := wt_mn == wt_mn[which.max(wt_mn)],
    by = c('ID1', 'ID2', splitBy)
  ]

  data.table::setnames(
    out,
    c('wt_mn', 'del'),
    c('direction_cor', 'direction_delay')
  )

  return(out)
}

#' Deprecated: leader_edge_delay
#'
#' The function `leader_edge_delay` has been renamed to
#' `leader_direction_delay`, with the original name deprecated to clarify the
#' distinction between the global directional correlation delay method decsribed
#' by Nagy et al. 2010 and implemented in `leader_direction_delay` and the
#' momentary directional correlation delay method described in Quera et al. 2023
#' and implemented in `edge_delay`.
#'
#' Previous versions of {spatsoc} included an incorrect translation of the
#' methods described in Nagy et al. 2010, specifically:
#'
#' - a misunderstanding regarding the \eqn{<...>} notation to represent
#' time-averaging in the figure caption of Nagy et al. 2010
#'
#'- the use of minimum differences in direction instead of the cosine of the
#' differences in direction (equivalent to the dot product of directions)
#'
#' - not returning the correlation in direction for thresholding or evaluation by
#' the user
#'
#' The previous version (`leader_edge_delay`) used the results of `edge_delay`
#' as an aggregation step. The renaming here is intended to differentiate these
#' functions as related, but not steps in a shared workflow: the user is not
#' expected to use the output from `edge_delay` in `leader_direction_delay`. As
#' above, `edge_delay` is the momentary directional correlation delay calculated
#' at each time step while `leader_direction_delay` is the global directional
#' correlation delay calculated for each dyad, and optionally sampling period
#' (using the 'splitBy' argument).
#'
#' @seealso [leader_direction_delay()]
#' @seealso [edge_delay()]
#'
#' @returns
#' leader_edge_delay is deprecated, see ?leader_edge_delay and NEWS for details
#' @export
#'
#' @examples
#' # leader_edge_delay is deprecated, see ?leader_edge_delay
leader_edge_delay <- function() {
  stop(
    'leader_edge_delay has been renamed to leader_direction_delay and refactored to',
    ' better follow methods described in Nagy et al. 2010.',
    '\nPlease see NEWS and help page ?leader_edge_delay for more details.'
  )
}
